#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c606cf5-2457-5244-a905-93c19f3cb6c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298128} {:answer/id #uuid "52b2d2c8-64fb-5367-aec2-909927a912d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298129} {:answer/id #uuid "bb23c4b5-cb59-55e0-89d8-86263dbdf556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298130} {:answer/id #uuid "cd923881-c910-5b58-8665-f91af3cf6340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298131} {:answer/id #uuid "0e115e35-2205-572a-a47e-d2fc46832408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e115e35-2205-572a-a47e-d2fc46832408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298132} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a6e3706-6942-5eff-9b6e-f3fe5fee27eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80e64203-3ebf-520c-9458-47cebefc9b47" :question/math-academy-id "q-131068" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[2,4]$\n- $[0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2x)=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[2,4].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,4].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[0,1)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "820a07b9-3b23-5ed1-a667-91c620241378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175009} {:answer/id #uuid "c27dc2ee-ad26-52d1-af45-ef0cf5e32788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175010} {:answer/id #uuid "f7cdfa59-ab0b-5361-830b-ff820723b8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175011} {:answer/id #uuid "0af0b6e7-7922-55cf-86d4-c495aa0ecaa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175012} {:answer/id #uuid "0d39d8cf-7121-56ba-ba3f-a30cef30fc30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "820a07b9-3b23-5ed1-a667-91c620241378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175009} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b580b14-5b39-5ecd-b84e-8a35135127f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e9ff6e93-5783-568e-aaac-4c58f2e3a7ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-47779" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-1, & x<0.5 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2}, & x\\ge 0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $(0,1)$\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[1,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(0,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0.5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0.5}(x-1)=(0.5)-1=-0.5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0.5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0.5}(4-{x}^{2})=4-(0.5)^{2}=3.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0.5$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.5.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[1,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0.5,$ which is not in the interval $[1,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b707f083-85e8-55fa-b4ec-94c17162148b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175027} {:answer/id #uuid "d5581514-6150-5c93-83f5-5f001de14370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175028} {:answer/id #uuid "ecce34d6-483b-50b6-aa0e-d84fe3702e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175029} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9b0f3f-4c7e-5d9a-bf86-cb95fea76b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175030} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba7d08e-9e3d-5771-8aab-1c658ad0c24d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecce34d6-483b-50b6-aa0e-d84fe3702e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175029} :answer-field/id #uuid "aac46546-e018-5ee1-bd15-137dc5071a34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ee96606-8ba8-569e-8f90-21918ad24c63" :question/math-academy-id "q-47787" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={log}_{2}⁡x?$ \n\n- $(0,5]$\n- $[1,5]$\n- $[0,5]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,5]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,5]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[0,5]$ because this interval contains points where the function is not defined. In particular, it is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daf06b6c-0378-5ed2-8e42-a98b24450905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298135} {:answer/id #uuid "f04b233e-7483-5e00-bb08-e8bf3fbefe36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298136} {:answer/id #uuid "a13aa3bd-0364-5cf0-951f-83c0135be641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298137} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc301fd-9324-5890-b98d-337dee9f4037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298138} {:answer/id #uuid "a198b3d6-9f9f-5e75-870c-bcef806c8cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a198b3d6-9f9f-5e75-870c-bcef806c8cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298139} :answer-field/id #uuid "a988c3b3-2492-5d62-88fc-547620a62e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d2b162e-d0d6-5092-9ce3-d107896e0e87" :question/math-academy-id "q-73482" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+1}}?$   \n\n- $[-1,0]$\n- $[1,10]$\n- $(-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,0]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=-1.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,10]$ is  closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval  $(-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c6339ba-a624-523e-b671-4a7097c207d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298142} {:answer/id #uuid "55502a97-916f-5735-b373-1d79779e47ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298143} {:answer/id #uuid "16f7c429-e668-5ac7-9181-1fc29f5d86ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298144} {:answer/id #uuid "036ed94f-c1c2-5874-a463-8ac34e75620a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298145} {:answer/id #uuid "ca006243-306d-5fcd-ad48-28dcaa147d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca006243-306d-5fcd-ad48-28dcaa147d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298146} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ac6ef48-91db-5697-aaba-750f72ac0b5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78156bf9-5946-5f89-b672-be4ba96fe937" :question/math-academy-id "q-73499" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1), & x>1 \\\\ x-1, & x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[-1,0]$\n- $[-1,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,0].$ In this interval, $f(x)=x-1$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,0].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, in the interval $[-1,2]$, we also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,2]$ and therefore  the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}]}
